# XSH hcreate(), hdestroy() and hsearch()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# All three are XSI. There is one table at a time. When the table counts as
# full is not defined (the size is only an estimate that may be adjusted), so
# the "table is full" result is not tested.

assertion  XSH.hcreate.001
anchor     functions/hcreate.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/hcreate.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       hcreate(50) returns non-zero, and 50 hsearch(ENTER) calls with
           distinct keys each return a non-null entry whose key is equal to
           the key given and whose data pointer is the one given.
test       t/001-create-enter.c

assertion  XSH.hcreate.002
anchor     functions/hcreate.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/hcreate.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       hsearch(FIND) of an entered key returns an entry with that key and
           the data entered, also when the key is given in another buffer
           (comparison is by string content); of an absent key it returns a
           null pointer and enters nothing.
test       t/002-find.c

assertion  XSH.hcreate.003
anchor     functions/hcreate.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       hsearch(ENTER) of a key that is already in the table returns the
           entry already there: the same location as hsearch(FIND) gives, and
           the table does not gain a second entry for it.
test       t/003-enter-existing.c

assertion  XSH.hcreate.004
anchor     functions/hcreate.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/hcreate.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       After hdestroy() another hcreate() succeeds and gives an empty
           table: keys entered before are not found, and new ones can be
           entered; this holds for three create/destroy cycles.
test       t/004-destroy-recreate.c
